<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dénia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Denia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dénia is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In the 11th century it was the capital of the maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mujahid-al-amiri">Mujahid al-Amiri</a>, which ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and was known for Quranic scholarship. It fell to the Crown of Aragon in 1244 and later gave its title to the marquises of Dénia, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma">Duke of Lerma</a>, favourite of Philip III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denia.jpg" alt="Dénia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denia_desde_el_Montg%C3%B3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
